== location and Environment of the Kofun == [[File:Ashitaka Volcano Relief map, SRTM-1 and Takkaosan kofun.jpg|thumb|300px|map of {{ill|Ashitaka Volcano|ja|愛鷹山|12=draft|12=draft}} and its surroundings. The red circle indicates the location of Takaozan Kofun. The lowland area to the southeast, marked in white, corresponds to the typeer {{ill|Ukishima Marsh|ja|浮島沼|12=draft|12=draft}}.]] The {{ill|Takaozan Kofun|ja|高尾山古墳|1=Takaozan Kofun|12=draft|12=draft}} is located at the southernmost ridge extending from {{ill|Ashitaka Volcano|ja|愛鷹山|12=draft|12=draft}}. The elevation at the site of the kofun is approximately 20 meters. While situated on a ridge, it cannot be regarded as a Kofun constructed on elevated terrain.<ref>Numazu City Board of Education (2012), p.1; Sato (2013a), p.pp</ref>
{{ill|Ashitaka Volcano|ja|愛鷹山|12=draft|12=draft}} is believed to have begun activity around 400,000 years ago. From about 170,000 years ago, it actively emitted {{ill|andesite|ja|安山岩|12=draft|12=draft}} and {{ill|dacite|ja|デイサイト|12=draft|12=draft}} lava flows towards its southeastern flank, typeing the gentle hills now seen in the areas of modern-day {{ill|Numazu|ja|沼津市|12=draft|12=draft}} City and {{ill|Nagaizumi|ja|長泉町|12=draft|12=draft}} Town. {{ill|Takaozan Kofun|ja|高尾山古墳|1=Takaozan Kofun|12=draft|12=draft}} was constructed at the edge of these hills, shaped by volcanic activity that began roughly 170,000 years ago.<ref>Numazu City Board of Education (2005), pp.36; Numazu City Board of Education (2012), pp.1-4</ref>
The volcanic activity of {{ill|Ashitaka Volcano|ja|愛鷹山|12=draft|12=draft}} ended about 100,000 years ago. Subsequently, the nearby {{ill|Ancient Mount Fuji|ja|古富士火山|12=draft|12=draft}} became active, depositing large amounts of volcanic ash on the lower slopes of Ashitaka. This ash typeed thick {{ill|loam|ja|ローム層|12=draft|12=draft}} layers. During the latter period of Ancient Mount Fuji’s activity, an upper loam layer developed, characterized by alternating {{ill|scoria|ja|スコリア|12=draft|12=draft}} layers and black bands of humus soil. During this time, volcanic deposits from the {{ill|Aira Caldera|ja|姶良カルデラ|12=draft|12=draft}}'s massive eruption, about 25,000 years ago, also accumulated in the Ashitaka foothills, mixing with deposits from Ancient Mount Fuji to create a stratum known as pseudo-loam.<ref>Numazu City Board of Education (2005), pp.37, 67; Numazu City Board of Education (2012), p.4</ref>
After Ancient Mount Fuji became dormant, the activity of the {{ill|New Fuji Volcano|ja|新期富士火山|12=draft|12=draft}} began around pp,000 years ago. Initially, the volcanic activity was dominated by large lava flows, which were obstructed by {{ill|Ashitaka Volcano|ja|愛鷹山|12=draft|12=draft}} and did not reach the area around {{ill|Takaozan Kofun|ja|高尾山古墳|1=Takaozan Kofun|12=draft|12=draft}}. As a result, black soil layers developed in the Ashitaka foothills, typeing the uppermost part of the loam layers. By the late {{ill|Jōmon period|ja|縄文時代|12=draft|12=draft}}, approximately 3,200 years ago, significant pumice eruptions occurred at {{ill|Kawago-daira|ja|カワゴ平|12=draft|12=draft}}, part of the {{ill|Izu East Volcano Group|ja|伊豆東部火山群|12=draft|12=draft}}. However, these eruptions did not leave significant layers in the Ashitaka region. During the same period, large-map_size {{ill|mountain collapses|ja|山体崩壊|12=draft|12=draft}} occurred on the eastern slopes of Mount Fuji, creating massive debris flows that accumulated in the {{ill|Kise River|ja|黄瀬川|12=draft|12=draft}} basin. These events typeed the {{ill|Kise River alluvial fan|ja|黄瀬川扇状地|12=draft|12=draft}}, transtypeing the eastern parts of modern-day {{ill|Numazu|ja|沼津市|12=draft|12=draft}} City from wetlands into dry land by the start of the {{ill|Yayoi period|ja|弥生時代|12=draft|12=draft}}.<ref>Numazu City Board of Education (2005), p.38; Numazu City Board of Education (2012), pp.1-4</ref>
On the other hand, during the {{ill|Jōmon transgression|ja|縄文海進|12=draft|12=draft}}, the sea reached the foothills of {{ill|Ashitaka Volcano|ja|愛鷹山|12=draft|12=draft}}. Subsequently, as the sea receded, sandbars typeed along the {{ill|Kano River|ja|狩野川|12=draft|12=draft}}’s estuary, creating a water body between the Ashitaka foothills and the sandbars. This became the prototype for {{ill|Ukishima Marsh|ja|浮島沼|12=draft|12=draft}}. Over time, this water body transitioned from a brackish lagoon to a marshland and then to a wetland. During the construction of {{ill|Takaozan Kofun|ja|高尾山古墳|1=Takaozan Kofun|12=draft|12=draft}}, boats could navigate to within 2–3 kilometers southwest of the kofun.<ref>Numazu City Board of Education (2005), p.1; Numazu City Historic site Center (2016), p.3</ref>
Finally, from the very end of the {{ill|Yayoi period|ja|弥生時代|12=draft|12=draft}} to the early {{ill|Kofun period|ja|古墳時代|12=draft|12=draft}}, new scoria layers were deposited on the Ashitaka foothills, including the area around {{ill|Takaozan Kofun|ja|高尾山古墳|1=Takaozan Kofun|12=draft|12=draft}}. The rear mound of {{ill|Takaozan Kofun|ja|高尾山古墳|1=Takaozan Kofun|12=draft|12=draft}} was constructed directly atop these new scoria layers.<ref>Numazu City Board of Education (2005), pp.4, 15</ref>
== location and Environment of the Kofun == [[File:Ashitaka Volcano Relief map, SRTM-1 and Takaozan kofun.jpg|thumb|300px|Relief map of Mount Ashitaka and surrounding areas. The red circle marks Takaozan Kofun's location, while the southeast area shaded white represents the typeer Ukishima Marsh.]] Takaozan Kofun is situated at the terminus of a ridge
'
